## P1 privacy / authorization hardening

The live GPS mutation path acquired a global pessimistic lock on a private job before proving that the authenticated caller was the assigned worker. That left the most sensitive tracking write surface inconsistent with participant-scoped reads and the checkpoint hardening merged in #209.

## Final change

- fail closed before repository access when the authenticated principal has no persisted user id;
- acquire the job through `findAssignedWorkerByIdForUpdate(jobId, workerId)` before any tracking-state read;
- return the same neutral `404` for a missing job and every non-assigned GPS publisher, including the job owner;
- only evaluate tracking lifecycle after the assigned-worker-scoped lock succeeds;
- preserve tracking-row locking, out-of-order protection, GPS quality/speed guards, ETA refresh behavior and terminal cleanup semantics;
- align runtime smoke with the neutral-not-found mutation privacy boundary.

## Regression coverage

Focused service-level tests prove that an unassigned publisher never reaches the global job lock or tracking state, a principal without a persisted id fails before any repository access, and lifecycle disclosure occurs only after the assigned-worker-scoped lock succeeds.

An earlier exact-head smoke run correctly exposed the stale owner-`403` expectation. The branch now keeps one coherent fail-closed `404` contract in both implementation and runtime verification. Because the head changed during the correction, only checks for the final current SHA are valid for merge.

## Base / merge gate

The branch originated from `master` `d5bf27427fbac42d4c9c5abd23d376367b592687` after #209 post-merge CI, CodeQL and Container Security were green.

Do not merge unless every relevant check is green for the exact current PR head, review threads are clear, and `master` is revalidated immediately before merge. Merge with a merge commit only.

